  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three ways displays can be integrated into textiles. At the top, a thin-film display is shown as a flexible sheet placed on fabric. One panel shows it lying flat on a woven textile, while another suggests that when the surrounding fabric is stretched, the rigid or less stretchable display deforms differently and may experience strain or cracking. This emphasizes the mechanical mismatch between conventional thin-film electronics and soft textiles.\n\nThe middle row presents a luminescent or optical fibre approach. A fibre cross-section is drawn with anode, cathode, and a light-emitting layer wrapped into the fibre, indicating that the fibre itself can emit light. When such fibres are woven into cloth, they can form patterns that illuminate within the fabric.\n\nThe bottom row shows an interwoven display architecture, where conductive and transparent electrode fibres cross with a luminescent layer at intersections. These crossing points act as pixels, creating a dense pixel array across the textile. When selected pixels are activated, they produce a visible image or characters. Overall, the figure compares sheet-based, fibre-based, and fully interwoven textile display strategies, highlighting increasing compatibility with fabric structure.",
